      <data key="d5">7</data>
      <data key="d6">350</data>
      <data key="d7">b0ac84e1a4fe4f32b7f191bd69b328ff</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GLUCEMIA">
      <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
      <data key="d1">GLUCEMIA refers to the concentration of glucose in the blood, which is a crucial factor in managing diabetes. Blood glucose levels were regularly monitored during the patient's treatment for diabetes and malnutrition, highlighting the importance of tracking these levels for effective management. In particular, glucemia is significant in the context of diabetes management, as it is influenced by dietary intake and can vary based on individual health conditions. This is especially relevant for patients with specific syndromes, such as MELAS syndrome, where blood sugar levels play a key role in overall health. Notably, the patient's blood sugar levels were critically high during her re-admission in May 2016, underscoring the need for careful monitoring and management of glucemia in diabetic patients.</data>
      <data key="d2">3dfc700a2eaec232c05b0a53f2089229,3ed179f6e734d9fe4921fb0be0b018e4,3f685a05a9682db6dcde958d1918cc77,b7a2c1e4dd77f47605fdb33f4a2c2bdf,e3370b71146ad5063d6b1e9c3d8a3bd2</data>
