    </node>
    <node id="NEUROLOGICAL SYMPTOMS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Neurological symptoms associated with MNGIE include peripheral neuropathy, ptosis, ophthalmoplegia, and leukoencephalopathy.</data>
      <data key="d2">cb3d17a78ad2d9342ebe0fd2aac8e9d1</data>
      <data key="d3">2</data>
      <data key="d4">0</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">471</data>
      <data key="d7">82ab07ad6b7744118da528fcaa44b43b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DIGESTIVE SYMPTOMS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Digestive symptoms of MNGIE include gastrointestinal dysmotility, early satiety, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain.</data>
      <data key="d2">cb3d17a78ad2d9342ebe0fd2aac8e9d1</data>
      <data key="d3">2</data>
      <data key="d4">0</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">472</data>
      <data key="d7">4b5c2b89dea446339545d4a6d2fe2469</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAQUEXIA">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">CAQUEXIA is a significant medical condition characterized by involuntary weight loss and muscle mass loss. It is particularly prevalent in patients suffering from Mitochondrial Neurogastrointestinal Encephalomyopathy (MNGIE), where it arises due to severe digestive symptoms. Additionally, cachexia is commonly observed in patients with advanced cancer, notably in those with adenocarcinoma of the pancreas. This condition not only contributes to malnutrition but also poses a considerable risk for further nutritional deficiencies. Overall, cachexia is a critical factor influencing the prognosis of affected individuals, especially in the context of cancer-related illnesses.</data>
      <data key="d2">5549895a3a7f54651e6c54f5a5d7d833,5f4610a3d5efde69d7f34ec14c4bed0b,cb3d17a78ad2d9342ebe0fd2aac8e9d1,da58ef987e74d508eb95395230f21cdc</data>
      <data key="d3">2</data>
      <data key="d4">0</data>
      <data key="d5">8</data>
      <data key="d6">473</data>
      <data key="d7">ba7aa6aaa5dd42ba9c84a7db129913f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ANALYTICAL FINDINGS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Analytical findings in MNGIE include elevated levels of thymidine and deoxyuridine in plasma, which are characteristic of the disorder.</data>
      <data key="d2">cb3d17a78ad2d9342ebe0fd2aac8e9d1</data>
      <data key="d3">2</data>
      <data key="d4">0</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">474</data>
      <data key="d7">e9c1299868b24e97bc5174994765162d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEUROIMAGING FINDINGS">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Neuroimaging findings in MNGIE typically show diffuse alterations in white matter, indicative of leukoencephalopathy.</data>
      <data key="d2">cb3d17a78ad2d9342ebe0fd2aac8e9d1</data>
      <data key="d3">2</data>
      <data key="d4">0</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">475</data>
      <data key="d7">cdaf1b88fbb744c19d04d0010d1df8af</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENETIC SEQUENCING">
      <data key="d0">EVENT</data>
      <data key="d1">Genetic sequencing is performed to identify mutations in the TYMP gene, confirming the diagnosis of MNGIE.</data>
      <data key="d2">cb3d17a78ad2d9342ebe0fd2aac8e9d1</data>
      <data key="d3">2</data>
      <data key="d4">0</data>
      <data key="d5">1</data>
      <data key="d6">476</data>
      <data key="d7">8ac37137ce664b948ab351e28c6dcc0d</data>
    </node>
